\section{Pattern surjectivity}

\begin{theorem}[Surjectivity onto sign patterns]\label{thm:surj}
Let $A$ be a duplicate-free probe list and $\varepsilon:A\to\{\pm1\}$ any prescribed
sign assignment. Then infinitely many primes $q$ satisfy
$\jac{a}{q}=\varepsilon(a)$ for all $a\in A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\emph{Stage 1: a modulus with the prescribed pattern.} The moduli $8$ and the odd
probes are pairwise coprime; prescribe by the Chinese remainder theorem the residue
$1$ or $5$ modulo $8$ according as the target value of $\jac{2}{\cdot}$ is $+1$ or
$-1$, and modulo each odd probe $a$ the residue $1$ if $\varepsilon(a)=+1$ and a
quadratic nonresidue mod $a$ if $\varepsilon(a)=-1$ (such a nonresidue exists in
every finite field of odd order). Let $m$ solve the system. Then $m\equiv 1\pmod 4$,
so the friendly case of quadratic reciprocity gives $\jac{a}{m}=\jac{m}{a}$, a
Legendre symbol depending only on $m\bmod a$, which the prescription controls; the
value at $2$ is read off from $m\bmod 8$ by the supplementary law. Nonvanishing of
all symbols forces $m$ coprime to all probes.

\emph{Stage 2: from modulus to primes.} Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:realize} to $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact range]\label{cor:range}
For a duplicate-free probe list of length $K$,
$\{\FA(q): q \text{ prime},\, q\notin A\}=\{\pm1\}^K$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exact leakage count]\label{thm:count}
$\bigl|\{\FA(q): q \text{ prime},\, q\notin A\}\bigr|=2^K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The set of $\pm1$-vectors of length $K$ has $2^K$ elements, by induction: vectors of
length $K+1$ are the disjoint union of those of length $K$ prefixed by $+1$ and by
$-1$, both prefix maps being injective. Combine with Corollary~\ref{cor:range}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No individual pinning]\label{cor:pinning}
Fix the observation $\FA(N_0)$ and a probe $a_0\in A$. There exist consistent
factorizations $p_1q_1$ and $p_2q_2$, both with fingerprint $\FA(N_0)$, such that
$\jac{a_0}{p_1}=+1$ and $\jac{a_0}{p_2}=-1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:surj} choose a prime $p_1$ with all probe symbols $+1$ and a
prime $p_2$ with symbol $-1$ at $a_0$ and $+1$ elsewhere; both lie outside $A$ since
their symbols are nonzero. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:nopruning} to each.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact structure: coset and torsor}

\begin{theorem}[Exact consistency criterion]\label{thm:consistency}
Let $p,q$ be primes with $p\notin A$. Then
\[
\FA(pq)=\FA(N_0)\iff \jac{a}{q}=\jac{a}{N_0}\jac{a}{p}\ \text{ for all } a\in A.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Entrywise, consistency says $\jac{a}{p}\jac{a}{q}=\jac{a}{N_0}$; since $p\notin A$
and both are prime, $\jac{a}{p}=\pm1$ squares to $1$ and may be moved across the
equation in either direction.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Sign group, anti-diagonal, fibre]
Equip $\{\pm1\}^K$ with the entrywise product $\odot$, an elementary abelian
$2$-group of order $2^K$; let $\Delta^-=\{(w,w):w\in\{\pm1\}^K\}$ act on
$\{\pm1\}^K\times\{\pm1\}^K$ by simultaneous multiplication. The
\emph{factorization fibre} of the observation is
\[
\Phi(N_0)=\bigl\{(\FA(p),\FA(q)) : p,q \text{ prime},\ p,q\notin A,\ \FA(pq)=\FA(N_0)\bigr\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The fibre is a coset]\label{thm:coset}
For a duplicate-free probe list and odd $N_0$ coprime to the probes,
\[
\Phi(N_0)=\bigl\{(u,\ \FA(N_0)\odot u) : u\in\{\pm1\}^K\bigr\},
\]
a coset of $\Delta^-$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\subseteq$) For consistent primes $p,q\notin A$, Corollary~\ref{cor:range} puts
$\FA(p)\in\{\pm1\}^K$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency} gives
$\FA(q)=\FA(N_0)\odot\FA(p)$. ($\supseteq$) Given $u$, Theorem~\ref{thm:surj}
produces a prime $p\notin A$ with $\FA(p)=u$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:nopruning} a
compensator $q$; its symbols are products of $\pm1$s, hence nonzero, so $q\notin A$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trivial torsor]\label{thm:torsor}
For any $x,y\in\Phi(N_0)$ there is a \emph{unique} $w\in\{\pm1\}^K$ with
$y=(w\odot x_1,\ w\odot x_2)$. Hence $\Delta^-$ acts simply transitively on
$\Phi(N_0)$: the fibre is a trivial $\Delta^-$-torsor.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Existence: take $w=y_1\odot x_1$; then $w\odot x_1=y_1$, and since both second
coordinates are $\FA(N_0)$ times the respective first (Theorem~\ref{thm:coset}),
$w\odot x_2=y_2$. Uniqueness: multiplying $y_1=w\odot x_1$ by $x_1$ recovers $w$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No pruning, geometric form]\label{thm:proj}
$\mathrm{pr}_1(\Phi(N_0))=\{\pm1\}^K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact fibre size]\label{thm:fibresize}
$|\Phi(N_0)|=2^K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Phi(N_0)$ is the image of $\{\pm1\}^K$ under the injective map
$u\mapsto(u,\FA(N_0)\odot u)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:count}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The leakage ledger]
Theorem~\ref{thm:count}: the channel distinguishes exactly $2^K$ values of $N$, so
it emits $K$ bits about $N$. Theorems~\ref{thm:proj} and \ref{thm:fibresize}:
conditioned on the observation, the fingerprint of the factor ranges over all $2^K$
possibilities, so the channel emits $0$ bits about the factorization. These two
numbers are the residue-leakage curve in full.
\end{remark}

\section{The sharp boundary}

\begin{lemma}[Visible degeneracy]\label{lem:degen}
If a probe prime $a$ divides $N_0\neq 0$ then $\jac{a}{N_0}=0$: the degenerate case
is visible directly in the observed data.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Probe divisor forces the factor]\label{thm:boundary}
Let $a\in A$ divide $N_0\neq 0$, let $p$ be a prime with $p\neq a$, and let $q$ be a
prime with $\FA(pq)=\FA(N_0)$. Then $q=a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $a$ the observation is $0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:degen}, so
$\jac{a}{p}\jac{a}{q}=0$; since $a\neq p$ are distinct primes, $\jac{a}{p}=\pm1$,
hence $\jac{a}{q}=0$, forcing $\gcd(a,q)\neq 1$ and thus $q=a$.
\end{proof}

Thus when a probe divides the target the channel prunes completely --- but this is
exactly the case in which trial division by that probe has already produced the
factor. The coprimality hypothesis cannot be dropped, and dropping it buys nothing.

\section{Scope of the obstruction}

\subsection{Arbitrary abelian channels}

\begin{definition}[Character fingerprint]
Let $R$ be a commutative monoid with zero, $M\ge 1$, and $\chi_1,\dots,\chi_K$
Dirichlet characters modulo $M$ with values in $R$. The \emph{character
fingerprint} of $N$ is $\Phi_\chi(N)=(\chi_1(N),\dots,\chi_K(N))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Abelian no-pruning]\label{thm:abelian}
For any such family, any $N_0$ coprime to $M$, and any $p$ coprime to $M$
(primality of $p$ is not required), there are infinitely many primes $q$ with
$\Phi_\chi(pq)=\Phi_\chi(N_0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $u,v\in(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ be the classes of $p$ and $N_0$. Dirichlet's
theorem supplies infinitely many primes $q$ in the unit class $vu^{-1}$; for each
$i$,
\[
\chi_i(pq)=\chi_i(p)\chi_i(vu^{-1})=\chi_i\bigl(u\cdot vu^{-1}\bigr)=\chi_i(v)=\chi_i(N_0).
\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

No abelian residue channel of bounded conductor can eliminate a single candidate
prime factor. The quadratic case is the specialization in which each $\chi_i$ is
the Jacobi symbol $\jac{a_i}{\cdot}$ of conductor $4a_i$; there $p^{-1}\equiv p$ up
to squares, which is why the compensating class appeared as $N_0p$. Applying the
theorem to the single mod-$8$ quadratic character recovers no-pruning for
$\jac{2}{\cdot}$ with no use of reciprocity at all.

\subsection{Non-abelian (Artin-symbol) channels}

\begin{definition}
For $\sigma,p,q$ in a group $G$, say $(p,q)$ is \emph{class-compatible} with
$\sigma$ if there exist $x,y\in G$ with $x$ conjugate to $p$, $y$ conjugate to $q$,
and $xy=\sigma$. The relation depends on $p,q$ only through their conjugacy classes,
so it is a statement about $\Cl(G)\times\Cl(G)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No pruning in every group]\label{thm:nonab}
For every group $G$, target $\sigma$ and candidate $p$, the element $q=p^{-1}\sigma$
is class-compatible with $\sigma$; hence the fibre surjects onto all of $\Cl(G)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Torsor $\iff$ abelian]\label{thm:torsoriff}
For a group $G$ the following are equivalent: (i) for all $\sigma,p,q,q'$, if
$(p,q)$ and $(p,q')$ are class-compatible with $\sigma$ then $q\sim q'$;
(ii) $G$ is abelian.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(ii)$\Rightarrow$(i): in an abelian group conjugacy is equality, so
class-compatibility says exactly $q=p^{-1}\sigma$. (i)$\Rightarrow$(ii): suppose
$ab\neq ba$ and take $\sigma=p=a$. Then $q=1$ is compatible (via $x=a$, $y=1$), and
so is $q'=(bab^{-1})^{-1}a$ (via $x=bab^{-1}\sim a$). If $1\sim q'$ then $q'=1$, i.e.
$bab^{-1}=a$, contradicting $ab\neq ba$.
\end{proof}

In $S_3$ this is concrete: with $\sigma=p=(0\,1)$, both the identity and a $3$-cycle
arise as compensators, and they are not conjugate.

\begin{theorem}[Element-level fibre]\label{thm:eltfibre}
For any group $G$ and any $\sigma,p$,
$\bigl|\{(x,y)\in G\times G : x\sim p,\ xy=\sigma\}\bigr|=|C_p|$, where $C_p$ is the
conjugacy class of $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$x\mapsto(x,x^{-1}\sigma)$ is a bijection from $C_p$ onto the fibre.
\end{proof}

So no-pruning is not an artifact of commutativity --- it survives every
group-theoretic residue channel --- while the rigid $2^K$-torsor picture is exactly
as general as the abelian hypothesis; the dichotomy is created purely by the
passage to conjugacy classes.

\subsection{Sieve independence}

\begin{definition}[Residue filter, soundness]
A \emph{residue filter} is a predicate $P(v,p)$, read ``on observing fingerprint $v$,
keep $p$ as a possible prime factor''. It is \emph{sound} if for all distinct
admissible candidates $x,y$ we have $P\bigl(\FA(xy),x\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No sound residue filter prunes]\label{thm:filter}
Let $P$ be sound. Then for every odd $N_0$ coprime to the probes and every
admissible candidate $p$, $P\bigl(\FA(N_0),p\bigr)$ holds; equivalently the accepted
set equals the full admissible candidate set.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:nopruning} provides infinitely many compensating primes; removing
the finitely many degenerate ones ($q=2$, $q=p$, the probes) leaves an admissible
$q\neq p$ with $\FA(pq)=\FA(N_0)$. Soundness applied to the genuine semiprime $pq$
gives $P(\FA(pq),p)$, and rewriting the fingerprint gives $P(\FA(N_0),p)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cryptanalytic form]
A residue filter that rejects even one admissible candidate for one admissible
observation is necessarily unsound: it discards a true factorization.
\end{corollary}

Nothing about the internal workings of $P$ is used --- it need not even be
computable. This upgrades the development from ``this sieve fails'' to ``no sieve of
this shape can exist''.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Fingerprint evaluation.} Compute $\jac{a_i}{N}$ by the
reciprocity-driven Euclidean algorithm; cost $O(K\log^2\max(a_i,N))$ bit operations,
with no factorization of $N$.

\paragraph{Compensator search.} Given $N_0$, $A$ and a candidate prime $p$, set
$r=N_0p\bmod M_A$ and scan the progression $r,r+M_A,r+2M_A,\dots$ for a prime. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:class} the first prime found is a valid compensator; termination is
Dirichlet, the size bound is Linnik.

\paragraph{Pattern realization.} Given a target sign vector, build a modulus by the
Chinese remainder theorem (residue $1$ or $5$ mod $8$; residue $1$ or a least
quadratic nonresidue mod each odd probe) and scan its class modulo $M_A$ for a
prime. By Theorem~\ref{thm:surj} this always succeeds. Combining the last two
procedures constructs, for any observation and any prescribed pattern, an explicit
consistent factorization occupying that position of the fibre.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Discriminative power is not pruning power}
The motivating experiment found that a $20$-probe fingerprint separates $300$ random
semiprimes without collision and inferred that the channel must constrain the
factors. The observation is correct but weaker than it looks --- $2^{20}$ output
values make $300$ distinct outputs unremarkable, and the fingerprint is provably far
from injective (Propositions~\ref{prop:period},~\ref{prop:sqclass} and
Corollary~\ref{cor:nothash}). The inference fails structurally: the information
carried about $(p,q)$ is exactly the symmetric products $\jac{a}{p}\jac{a}{q}$, i.e.
exactly what one reads off $N$ itself. Recovering an individual symbol $\jac{a}{p}$
would require knowing $p$.

\subsection{A general lesson}
Suppose an invariant $I$ is cheap, multiplicative over the decomposition one wishes
to invert, and takes values in a bounded group. Then observing $I(N)$ determines the
product of the invariants of the parts and nothing else; since in a group every
element is a product of any prescribed first factor with a suitable second, no part
is excluded, provided a realization theorem guarantees the compensating class is
inhabited by legitimate objects. In our setting that theorem is Dirichlet's.

\subsection{Limits}
Three caveats: (i) Theorem~\ref{thm:abelian} fixes the modulus $M$, so channels of
growing conductor are not covered; (ii) all statements are hint-free, concerning the
fingerprint alone; (iii) the theorems rule out sound \emph{filters}, not every
conceivable randomized or amortized use of residue data --- but they do show such a
use cannot shrink the candidate set on its own.

\section{Future work}

\textbf{Effective no-pruning.} The arithmetic half is settled
(Theorems~\ref{thm:class},~\ref{thm:effective}); what remains is an explicit, small
bound for the least prime in a coprime class modulo $4\prod_{i\le K}p_i$, with
constants good enough for practice.

\textbf{Growing conductor.} Locate the exact threshold at which a channel of
growing conductor begins to prune.

\textbf{Beyond residues.} Formulate a general no-pruning criterion for multiplicative
invariants with a realization theorem; candidate settings include ideal-class data,
elliptic-curve reduction data, and Frobenius traces.

\textbf{Non-abelian multiplicity.} Quantify how the number of compensating classes
varies with the candidate class in a non-abelian group, and whether that variation
can ever be exploited statistically.

\textbf{Statistical leakage.} Conditioned on the observation, how are the
\emph{sizes} of consistent factor pairs distributed? Exclusion is impossible, but a
nontrivial posterior is not obviously ruled out.

\section{Conclusion}

The quadratic-residue fingerprint over $K$ probe primes is a cheap, well-behaved
arithmetic statistic that emits exactly $K$ bits about an integer $N$ and exactly
zero bits about the factorization of $N$. Every candidate prime factor survives the
observation, infinitely often; every sign pattern of the factor's own fingerprint
remains available; the set of consistent factor-fingerprint pairs is a coset of the
anti-diagonal on which the anti-diagonal acts simply transitively, of size exactly
$2^K$; the sole exception is the degenerate case in which a probe divides the
target, which trial division already handles. The phenomenon holds for every finite
family of Dirichlet characters of a fixed modulus and persists, as far as pruning is
concerned, even for non-abelian Artin-symbol data. Finally it is sieve-independent:
any candidate filter that never discards a true factorization accepts every
admissible candidate.

\medskip
\noindent Cheap residues identify. They do not factor.

